          "citation": "168 Ariz. 531, 815 P.2d 900 (1991)",
          "court": "Arizona Supreme Court",
          "year": 1991,
          "date_decided": "1991-07-25",
          "holding": "The Arizona Supreme Court vacated the superior court's disclosure order, holding that a document is not a public record merely because a public officer possesses it and that it must bear some relation to the officer's official duties, and ordering the tribal check distribution list returned.",
          "relevance": "The decision on which Griffis and ACLU v. DCS both expressly rest, supplying the three alternative definitions of 'public record' used in every Arizona transparency case.",
          "quote": "a public record must also have some relation to the official duties of the public officer that holds the record",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/1280889/salt-river-pima-maricopa-indian-community-v-rogers/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 1280889
        },
        {
          "case_name": "Carlson v. Pima County",
          "citation": "141 Ariz. 487, 687 P.2d 1242 (1984)",
          "court": "Arizona Supreme Court",
          "year": 1984,
          "date_decided": "1984-05-29",
          "holding": "The Arizona Supreme Court approved the court of appeals' decision and held that records a public officer is required to keep are presumed open to inspection, subject to the custodian's discretion to deny access where confidentiality, privacy, or the best interest of the state outweighs disclosure, with any such refusal subject to judicial scrutiny.",
          "relevance": "The origin of the presumption of disclosure Griffis quotes, and the case that sets out the statutory remedy for wrongful denial under A.R.S. § 39-121.02.",
          "quote": "all records required to be kept under A.R.S. § 39-121.01(B), are presumed open to the public for inspection as public records",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/1389401/carlson-v-pima-county/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 1389401
        },
        {
          "case_name": "American Civil Liberties Union of Arizona v. Arizona Department of Child Safety",
          "citation": "240 Ariz. 142, 377 P.3d 339 (App. 2016)",
          "court": "Court of Appeals of Arizona",
          "year": 2016,
          "date_decided": "2016-06-09",
          "holding": "The court of appeals held that the electronic records and data DCS maintains in its CHILDS case-management system are public records subject to Arizona's public records law, affirming in part, reversing in part, and remanding.",
          "relevance": "The Arizona case directly holding a child-welfare database to be a disclosable public record.",
          "quote": "Under any of the formulations of a public record discussed above, and based on its content, CHILDS is a public record.",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/6604368/american-civil-liberties-union-v-arizona-department-of-child-safety/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 6604368
        },
